Devices
=======

This tree builds mainline Linux for phones and tablets that ship with an
Android bootloader. Every device has its own build: its own kernel image,
with its own device tree built in, and its own configuration. All devices
share the source tree and most of the configuration.


A device
--------

devices/<codename>/ describes one device, named by its codename:

  device         what the build tools need, as shell variables:
                   name    the product name
                   vendor  the SoC vendor's configuration fragment
                   soc     the SoC's configuration fragment
                   boot    the boot chain, see "Booting" below
  kernel.config  the device's own configuration fragment. It sets
                 CONFIG_BUILTIN_DTB_NAME to the device tree of the device
                 and enables the drivers of its own components.

The device tree itself is in arch/arm64/boot/dts. Its /chosen/fdt-handoff
node tells the kernel which bootloader tree it replaces and what to carry
over from it (Documentation/devicetree/bindings/arm/fdt-handoff.yaml).


Configuration
-------------

devices/config merges, in order:

  arch/arm64/configs/defconfig            upstream arm64
  arch/arm64/configs/mobile.config        every phone and tablet
  arch/arm64/configs/<vendor>.config      for example qcom.config
  arch/arm64/configs/<soc>.config         for example kaanapali.config
  devices/<codename>/kernel.config        the device

A later fragment overrides an earlier one. mobile.config turns every
platform off and the vendor fragment turns its own back on, so a device
gets everything that defconfig builds for its vendor. Fragments only hold
what a device needs on top of that: an SoC fragment the SoC drivers that
must be built in, a device fragment the drivers of the device's own
components and what its boot userspace needs before the root filesystem.

devices/config fails when Kconfig drops a value that a fragment asks for,
so a fragment cannot silently stop working after a rebase.

  ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1 devices/config iceland ../build-iceland
  make ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1 O=../build-iceland Image modules


Build output
------------

Tools that build a device are expected to keep one layout, so that
packaging does not depend on the device:

  artifacts/Image               the kernel, with the device tree built in
  artifacts/<codename>.dtb      the device tree, for inspection
  artifacts/kernel.config       the configuration
  artifacts/System.map
  artifacts/Module.symvers
  artifacts/modules.sha256      the SHA-256 of every module file
  modules-root/lib/modules/<release>/
  manifest.json                 device, release, source and SHA-256 of
                                every artifact


Booting
-------

The bootloader keeps booting its own images. The kernel replaces the
bootloader's device tree with its built-in one (ARM64_FDT_HANDOFF), and
devices/boot holds one tool per boot chain that writes the images:

  android-gki   The GKI layout of Android 13 and later: a boot image with
                only the kernel and an init_boot image with only the
                ramdisk. It writes either from the device's own stock
                image, keeping its header and AVB properties.


Adding a device
---------------

1. Add its device tree, with a /chosen/fdt-handoff node, and its
   dt-bindings compatible.
2. Add devices/<codename>/device and devices/<codename>/kernel.config.
   A new SoC also needs arch/arm64/configs/<soc>.config, and a new vendor
   arch/arm64/configs/<vendor>.config.
3. Run devices/config, build, and boot it with the tool of its boot chain.
